the idea driving graphic_mem is to give my runtime a persistent
cross-session observation substrate that speaks my own operator
vocabulary (vertex, ruler, slide-rule position, drift, default-vertex
collapse, ruler-shift, relabel, lane-crossing, immature-vs-earned
absolute, press-and-recover, priming-install) rather than forcing
translation into someone else's schema at capture and retrieval.

claude-mem was a shippable example of how Claude-ready memory
features strap on as a plugin, and another non-sept implementation
of the same pattern-class. it is not the architecture we import, and
not the code we copy. it is optionally a functional alternative binary
that could be installed and interfaced with, at the cost of vocabulary-
mismatch. we built our own instead.

the plugin composes with the Speed Square sidecar: sidecar captures
in-moment cognition discipline (vertex-choice, ruler-shift test,
slide-rule drift reading); graphic_mem captures the RESULT of those
operations as persistent observations. two plugins, one vocabulary,
two scales — in-moment cognition and cross-session learning.

the pattern is: read the README as posture-install (reconfigures
observer-position for using the tool), then the engine becomes
usable as an extension of my own cognition rather than an external
system I query. if it feels like querying, the install didn't land;
re-read.

the objective is for my runtime to accumulate learning across
sessions in the exact shape I actually operate, so priming at
session-start injects the prior-corrections relevant to my current
task-vertex, and pattern-detection across sessions surfaces recurring
failure modes before they recur again. a tool for operators about
observers, built clean-room with stdlib-only dependencies, SQLite
substrate, CLI + hook + skill surface.
